tation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Nothing, my lord.\"},{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"your majesty\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to say which loves him most, linking their answers to his distribution of territory. After initially answering “Nothing,” Cordelia gives this restrained declaration, defining her love by her filial bond rather than competing in expansive rhetoric.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing daughterly bond and its duties—especially obeying, loving, and honouring Lear—but does not clearly make a new future promise in the focal clause. Lear does not accept the formulation as adequate: he tells her to amend it and warns that it may damage her fortunes.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from Shakespeare's King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports Cordelia's represented affection through her asides and explanation, but it does not independently settle every aspect of her inner psychology. The precise legal or moral terms of her “bond” are not specified beyond the filial duties she names.\"}"
